# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Template for the local .env file.
#
# Copy this file to .env (scripts/bootstrap.ps1 does it for you) and fill in
# the values on your workstation only. .env is ignored by Git and must never
# be committed, pasted into a ticket, or shared over chat.
#
# Rule enforced across this repository: configuration files declare the NAME
# of an environment variable, never its value. Values live here, or in the
# secret store of the pipeline that runs the automation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Azure DevOps connection (required by every automation) ----------------
# Organization URL, without a trailing slash.
ADO_ORG_URL=https://dev.azure.com/contoso
# Team project that holds the Teams, Boards, Variable Groups and connections.
ADO_PROJECT=Platform
# Personal Access Token. See docs/guides/getting-started.md for the minimum
# scopes and keep the shortest expiry that covers your change window.
ADO_PAT=

# --- Team membership (team-provisioning) -----------------------------------
# One variable per application. The value is a semicolon-separated list of
# user principal names that already exist in the organization.
APP_ALPHA_MEMBERS=
APP_BETA_MEMBERS=

# --- Variable Group values (variable-group-configuration) ------------------
# Non-secret values are NOT read from here. They are declared in the values
# file - automations/variable-group-configuration/config/variable-groups.csv -
# one row per application, environment and key, because a non-secret value is
# part of the declared state and belongs under version control with the rest
# of it. A row with an empty value is rejected rather than skipped: an empty
# value is not the same as "leave it alone", and the way to leave a key alone
# is to remove its row.
#
# Only secrets are read from the environment, and only because their value
# must never be committed. That is the whole of the split.

# Secret Variable Group values, needed to RE-POST a secret when the automation
# writes any non-secret key in the same group. A Variable Group PUT sends the
# whole object, so an omitted secret is stored as an empty string - which is why
# the automation refuses to write at all when it cannot resolve one.
#
# The name is ENVIRONMENT QUALIFIED: the secret APP_SERVER_PASSWORD in the PROD
# group is read from APP_SERVER_PASSWORD_PROD. That is deliberate. The group name
# carries the environment; the secret's own name does not, and the live value
# cannot be read back to compare against - so a single APP_SERVER_PASSWORD would
# let a DEV value be written over the PROD credential, with the API reporting
# success and nothing able to detect it afterwards.
#
# Leave one empty and the matching group is reported blocked, which is the safe
# outcome. Only pass -AllowUnqualifiedSecretName if one credential is genuinely
# shared across every environment.
APP_SERVER_PASSWORD_DEV=
APP_SERVER_PASSWORD_QA=
APP_SERVER_PASSWORD_PROD=

# --- Service Connection credentials (service-connection-provisioning) -----
# Secret values for SSH/SFTP endpoints. Leave empty to create the connection
# with the PENDING_OWNER_CONFIGURATION placeholder and let the platform owner
# complete it in the portal.
SFTP_APP_ALPHA_DEV_HOST=
SFTP_APP_ALPHA_DEV_USERNAME=
SFTP_APP_ALPHA_DEV_PASSWORD=
SFTP_APP_ALPHA_DEV_PRIVATE_KEY=
